/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Each line or branch gets two antiparallel SCRs, or a TRIAC at low power.
   - The SCRs are fired 60° apart in the order T1 (line a, positive), T2 (c, negative), T3 (b, positive), T4 (a, negative), T5 (c, positive), T6 (b, negative), with α measured from the zero crossing of phase a.
   - Vo is the RMS voltage across each load resistor and α is in radians.
   - Why zero at 150°?
   - With equal firing and a balanced load the line currents contain harmonics of order 6k ± 1: 5th, 7th, 11th, 13th.
   - Inside a delta each branch current has its own 3rd harmonic, but the line current is the difference of two branch currents, so the triplens cancel in the lines and circulate round the delta.
   - A soft starter begins at a large α (low voltage) and reduces α so the motor voltage ramps up with the current held at a set limit.
*/
