/* book exam traps: Applying the single-phase formula to one phase of a three-wire star. At α = 120° it gives 105.9 V on 415 V instead of the correct 49.8 V. | Writing 180° as the zero-output angle of a three-wire star. It is 150°; 180° holds for the four-wire star and for controllers inside a delta. | Forgetting double pulsing. With single short pulses the controller cannot work above α = 60°, because two SCRs in different lines must be on together. | Expecting triplen harmonics in the line currents of a balanced three-wire load. They circulate inside a delta or flow in a neutral, never in the three lines alone. */
