/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The power stage has two switches.
   - The switching frequency fsw is typically 5 kHz to 20 kHz for IGBTs.
   - Vo is the RMS of the chopped load voltage and V1 the RMS of its 50 Hz part, with Vs the RMS supply voltage.
   - The squared output is vs² for a fraction D of the time, which gives Vs√D.
   - Vo exceeds V1 by the sideband content.
   - With a resistor and no filter, the supply current is a pulse train of RMS Vs√D/R while the power is D·Vs²/R, so the power factor is √D.
   - Raising fsw lets the filter L and C shrink roughly as 1/fsw, but switching loss rises roughly in proportion to fsw.
   - Uses: AC voltage stabilisers, electronic tap changers and motor soft starting with a good input power factor.
*/
