/* book exam traps: Calling D·Vs the RMS output. It is the 50 Hz part; the RMS of the chopped wave is Vs√D, which is larger: 178.2 V against 138.0 V at D = 0.6. | Claiming the unfiltered supply current is sinusoidal. It is a pulse train with power factor √D; only the input filter makes the supply see a sine. | Using one IGBT with an antiparallel diode as the AC switch. It blocks only one polarity; two devices back to back are needed. | Writing that a plain dead time is safe with an inductive load. With both switches off, the load current has no path; the changeover must follow the voltage or current sign. */
